<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  bushfire_fleet.launch
  =====================
  Step 2 of 3: the three aircraft and the fire ground they work.

    roslaunch uav_bushfire bushfire_fleet.launch

  Expects a world already running - see bushfire_world.launch. Spawns the three
  quadrotors, their suppressors, and the fire_spotter that owns the fires, the
  claims and the ledger.

  Restartable, and that is the point of the split: changing a coalition
  parameter costs one relaunch of this file instead of reloading Gazebo. Every
  tuning number lives here, so a sweep needs no edit:

    roslaunch uav_bushfire bushfire_fleet.launch max_crew:=2
    roslaunch uav_bushfire bushfire_fleet.launch num_hotspots:=8

  Note that the aircraft are spawned by this file, so relaunching it into a
  world that still holds them fails the spawn with "entity already exists". The
  models are Gazebo's, not the launch's, and outlive it. Delete them first, or
  restart the world.
-->
<launch>


  <arg name="gui"      default="true" />
  <arg name="headless" default="false" />

  <!-- RViz shows the whole incident in one view: every fire with its intensity
       label, each aircraft, and the scan each one is steering by. Off by
       default because Gazebo is already drawing the scene and this machine
       would rather spend the frames on physics. -->
  <arg name="rviz"        default="false" />
  <arg name="rviz_config" default="$(find uav_bushfire)/rviz/bushfire.rviz" />

  <arg name="world" default="$(find uav_bushfire)/worlds/bushfire.world" />

  <!-- Fires burning at once. More than the fleet size on purpose: the choice
       each drone makes is only interesting if there is more than one thing
       worth choosing, and with three fires and three drones the answer is
       always "take the free one". Five means a drone regularly has to weigh a
       small fire it would own against a share of a big one. -->
  <arg name="num_hotspots" default="7" />

  <!-- Fires are lit anywhere inside +-world_half_size, and this is the number
       the whole scenario's scale follows from.

       It was 4.0, against a stand of eight trees standing in a ring around
       it - so every fire, every transit and every bombing run happened inside
       one 8 x 8 m square. That is a campfire with three drones over it, not a
       bushfire operation.

       At 6.0 the fire ground is 12 x 12 m, four times the area, with the
       trees scattered across all of it rather than ringing it. Three things
       have to move with it, and make_bushfire_world prints all three when it
       regenerates the world:
         - the geofence in one_uav.launch, which has to enclose it (12.8/16.0)
         - the graded break, which rings it from 12.6 m
         - num_hotspots, so the fires do not thin out over nine times the ground
       -->
  <arg name="world_half_size" default="6.0" />

  <!-- Suppression: horizontal radius AND altitude ceiling, both. The drone has
       to be over the fire and down at it.

       Both are wider than a single drone needs, because a crew does not stand
       on the fire - it stands on a ring around it, stacked in altitude. The
       radius has to contain the ring (0.71 m for a crew of three at 1.0 m
       separation) and the ceiling has to contain the stack (0.95 + 2 x 0.15 =
       1.25 for the top member), both with margin, or the geometry that keeps
       drones off each other would quietly stop them working. -->
  <arg name="collect_dist"      default="1.00" />
  <arg name="suppress_altitude" default="1.45" />
  <arg name="drop_altitude"     default="0.95" />

  <!-- What a fire costs, against what it is worth. Its intensity (1-10) is
       both: it pays that many points, split equally between every drone that
       chose it, and it takes intensity x work_per_intensity drone-seconds of
       bombing to put out, with every crew member overhead contributing a
       second of work per second.

       That pair is the whole mechanic. Payout alone makes joining somebody
       else's fire strictly worse than taking your own and no crew ever forms;
       work alone makes crews free and everybody piles onto one fire. Together,
       a drone maximises share per second - and the fire where that is best is
       the big one, which is how big fires draw crews without anything being
       told to make them. 1.2 puts a lone drone on a 10 for twelve seconds and
       a crew of three on it for four. -->
  <arg name="work_per_intensity" default="1.2" />

  <!-- Most drones one fire will take. Three is the fleet: the cap is really a
       geometric one - stations get closer as the ring fills - so raising it
       means raising crew_separation, collect_dist and the ceiling with it. -->
  <arg name="max_crew" default="3" />

  <!-- ...and a crew closes once it is this far through the job. Equal shares
       plus shared labour has exactly one degenerate corner: the time left in a
       fire collapses as it finishes, so a fire at 95% looks like the best
       thing on the board and joining it pays a full share for arriving after
       it is over. Measured on the first run of this - a drone bombed a 7 alone
       for fourteen seconds and was joined at 0.3 drone-seconds remaining by
       both others, keeping 2.33 of it. Only applies to a fire that already has
       a crew: taking over an abandoned job at 90% is fine, there is nobody to
       take it from. -->
  <arg name="join_deadline" default="0.7" />

  <!-- Crew geometry. Neighbours on the ring are kept this far apart and each
       member sits a step higher than the last, because two aircraft holding
       station on the same point is a collision and with crews that is the
       normal case rather than bad luck. -->
  <!-- 1.30, not 1.00. Two aircraft need 0.80 m centre to centre before their
       rotor discs even touch, so a designed separation of 1.00 left 0.20 m for
       every bit of station-keeping error - and put the ring inside the
       governor's own hard radius of 0.95, so the safety net was permanently
       engaged at exactly the place the scenario wants three drones to sit. -->
  <arg name="crew_separation"    default="1.30" />
  <arg name="crew_altitude_step" default="0.15" />

  <!-- A commitment is binding, so something has to break it when the aircraft
       cannot honour it - stuck in a thicket, or in a respawn loop. Long on
       purpose: this is a fault handler, not a way to change your mind. (A
       drone that gets rescued gives its fire up itself, immediately.) -->
  <arg name="commit_timeout" default="90.0" />

  <!-- ...and not *below* this, because "under the ceiling" is also true of an
       aircraft sitting on its landing gear. Without a floor, a fire lit near a
       launch point is scored by a drone that has not taken off: measured, two
       of thirty-one drops in a run were awarded at 0.18 m, four seconds before
       anybody was airborne. -->
  <arg name="min_drop_altitude" default="0.45" />

  <!-- A suppressed fire leaves the world and the board for this long before
       the slot lights somewhere else. Without the gap the model was moved
       straight to its next position, so a fire never went *out* from the
       viewer's side - it blinked from under three drones to across the arena,
       which reads as a fire that moved rather than one that was beaten. The
       pause is also what makes deleting and respawning the model safe rather
       than racy; see _extinguish() in fire_spotter. -->
  <arg name="extinguish_pause" default="1.5" />

  <!-- What a fire looks like. Its intensity drives the geometry - flame
       height, ember bed, smoke column and the radius its light throws - so a
       10 is a bonfire that lights the trunks around it and a 1 is a smoulder.
       flame_scale is a taste knob on top of that; fire_light is the single
       biggest cue, because it is the only part that illuminates anything else
       (emissive geometry lights nothing); flicker yaws the flames a few
       degrees at this rate so they move. Turn the light off, or flicker down,
       if the frame rate hurts on this machine. -->
  <arg name="flame_scale" default="1.0" />
  <arg name="fire_light"  default="true" />
  <arg name="flicker_hz"  default="4.0" />

  <!-- Where the trees are, so the spotter never lights a fire inside a trunk.
       It cannot see them.

       This was eight [x, y, 0.35] entries written out here by hand, with a
       comment telling you to keep them in step with worlds/bushfire.world and
       nothing checking that you had. At eight trunks that was merely fragile.
       The fire ground is 24 x 24 m now and the stand is thirty-odd trees
       scattered through it, so the list is GENERATED: scripts/make_bushfire_world
       writes config/obstacles.yaml in the same pass that places the trees, and
       this loads it. Nothing is duplicated, so nothing can drift.

       Regenerate both together:
         rosrun uav_bushfire make_bushfire_world

       Three nodes read it - fire_spotter to avoid lighting inside a trunk,
       fire_suppressor to govern its velocity near one, collision_monitor to
       score a strike. -->
  <arg name="obstacle_file"
       default="$(find uav_bushfire)/config/obstacles.yaml" />

  <!-- Transit altitude. Well above the suppression ceiling, because the gap
       between the two *is* the bombing run: at 1.6 the aircraft dipped barely
       half a metre and the drop was hard to see happening. Still well under
       the 3 m trunks, so the drones remain inside the timber and have to go
       round trees rather than over them. -->
  <!-- Lane 0. The other two fly cruise_step above it, giving 1.70 / 2.15 /
       2.60 - see lane_index in one_uav.launch. It was a single shared 2.0 m,
       which put all three aircraft permanently inside each other's separation
       bubble. -->
  <arg name="cruise_altitude" default="1.70" />
  <arg name="cruise_step" default="0.45" />
  <arg name="linear_speed"    default="1.2" />
  <arg name="angular_gain"    default="2.0" />

  <!-- Switch from flying to station-keeping inside this, measured to the
       drone's own station rather than to the fire. Kept tight because the
       station is now a slot other aircraft are standing next to: arriving
       loosely into a crew is how two of them meet. -->
  <arg name="arrive_radius" default="0.60" />

  <!-- Avoidance ranges are much wider than the TurtleBot3's 0.45/0.25: these
       drones cruise at 1.2 m/s against its 0.3, and stopping distance is what
       these numbers really encode. A multirotor also has to pitch back to
       decelerate, which costs distance a differential drive does not spend. -->
  <!-- Keeping the aircraft off each other, from known positions rather than
       from the laser. See _peer_filter in scripts/fire_suppressor. -->
  <arg name="rotor_radius" default="0.40" />
  <arg name="hard_margin" default="0.15" />
  <arg name="govern_lookahead" default="0.4" />
  <arg name="peer_radius" default="1.20" />
  <arg name="peer_vsep" default="0.35" />
  <arg name="peer_push" default="0.60" />

  <!-- Measures drone-drone and drone-tree separations and says which is
       actually happening. On by default: it only subscribes. -->
  <arg name="collide" default="true" />

  <arg name="avoid_dist" default="1.6" />
  <arg name="stop_dist"  default="0.9" />

  <!-- ============================ the aircraft ==========================
       Spread along the y axis in the clear middle of the stand, 2.2 m apart -
       comfortably more than a rotor span, so nobody starts inside anybody. -->

  <group ns="uav_0">
    <include file="$(find uav_bushfire)/launch/one_uav.launch">
      <arg name="ns" value="uav_0" />
      <arg name="lane_index" value="0" />
      <arg name="cruise_step" value="$(arg cruise_step)" />
      <arg name="obstacle_file" value="$(arg obstacle_file)" />
      <arg name="rotor_radius" value="$(arg rotor_radius)" />
      <arg name="hard_margin" value="$(arg hard_margin)" />
      <arg name="govern_lookahead" value="$(arg govern_lookahead)" />
      <arg name="x"  value="0.0" />
      <arg name="y"  value="0.0" />
      <arg name="cruise_altitude" value="$(arg cruise_altitude)" />
      <arg name="drop_altitude"   value="$(arg drop_altitude)" />
      <arg name="linear_speed"    value="$(arg linear_speed)" />
      <arg name="angular_gain"    value="$(arg angular_gain)" />
      <arg name="avoid_dist"      value="$(arg avoid_dist)" />
      <arg name="stop_dist"       value="$(arg stop_dist)" />
      <arg name="arrive_radius"   value="$(arg arrive_radius)" />
      <arg name="max_crew"        value="$(arg max_crew)" />
      <arg name="join_deadline"   value="$(arg join_deadline)" />
      <arg name="peer_radius"     value="$(arg peer_radius)" />
      <arg name="peer_vsep"       value="$(arg peer_vsep)" />
      <arg name="peer_push"       value="$(arg peer_push)" />
    </include>
  </group>

  <group ns="uav_1">
    <include file="$(find uav_bushfire)/launch/one_uav.launch">
      <arg name="ns" value="uav_1" />
      <arg name="lane_index" value="1" />
      <arg name="cruise_step" value="$(arg cruise_step)" />
      <arg name="obstacle_file" value="$(arg obstacle_file)" />
      <arg name="rotor_radius" value="$(arg rotor_radius)" />
      <arg name="hard_margin" value="$(arg hard_margin)" />
      <arg name="govern_lookahead" value="$(arg govern_lookahead)" />
      <arg name="x"  value="0.0" />
      <arg name="y"  value="2.2" />
      <arg name="cruise_altitude" value="$(arg cruise_altitude)" />
      <arg name="drop_altitude"   value="$(arg drop_altitude)" />
      <arg name="linear_speed"    value="$(arg linear_speed)" />
      <arg name="angular_gain"    value="$(arg angular_gain)" />
      <arg name="avoid_dist"      value="$(arg avoid_dist)" />
      <arg name="stop_dist"       value="$(arg stop_dist)" />
      <arg name="arrive_radius"   value="$(arg arrive_radius)" />
      <arg name="max_crew"        value="$(arg max_crew)" />
      <arg name="join_deadline"   value="$(arg join_deadline)" />
      <arg name="peer_radius"     value="$(arg peer_radius)" />
      <arg name="peer_vsep"       value="$(arg peer_vsep)" />
      <arg name="peer_push"       value="$(arg peer_push)" />
    </include>
  </group>

  <group ns="uav_2">
    <include file="$(find uav_bushfire)/launch/one_uav.launch">
      <arg name="ns" value="uav_2" />
      <arg name="lane_index" value="2" />
      <arg name="cruise_step" value="$(arg cruise_step)" />
      <arg name="obstacle_file" value="$(arg obstacle_file)" />
      <arg name="rotor_radius" value="$(arg rotor_radius)" />
      <arg name="hard_margin" value="$(arg hard_margin)" />
      <arg name="govern_lookahead" value="$(arg govern_lookahead)" />
      <arg name="x"  value="0.0" />
      <arg name="y"  value="-2.2" />
      <arg name="cruise_altitude" value="$(arg cruise_altitude)" />
      <arg name="drop_altitude"   value="$(arg drop_altitude)" />
      <arg name="linear_speed"    value="$(arg linear_speed)" />
      <arg name="angular_gain"    value="$(arg angular_gain)" />
      <arg name="avoid_dist"      value="$(arg avoid_dist)" />
      <arg name="stop_dist"       value="$(arg stop_dist)" />
      <arg name="arrive_radius"   value="$(arg arrive_radius)" />
      <arg name="max_crew"        value="$(arg max_crew)" />
      <arg name="join_deadline"   value="$(arg join_deadline)" />
      <arg name="peer_radius"     value="$(arg peer_radius)" />
      <arg name="peer_vsep"       value="$(arg peer_vsep)" />
      <arg name="peer_push"       value="$(arg peer_push)" />
    </include>
  </group>


  <!-- ============================ fire ground ===========================
       One node, no namespace. It owns the fires and keeps the ledger - who is
       on what, how much work each fire has left, and who gets paid - but it no
       longer assigns anything: the drones choose and claim. Held back until
       Gazebo has the world and all three models loaded, since it spawns the
       flames itself and meters work by drone position, so starting it into a
       half-built simulator only produces timeouts to read. -->
  <node pkg="uav_bushfire" type="fire_spotter" name="fire_spotter"
        output="screen"
        launch-prefix="bash -c 'sleep 12 &amp;&amp; exec &quot;$0&quot; &quot;$@&quot;' --">
    <rosparam param="drones">[uav_0, uav_1, uav_2]</rosparam>
    <param name="num_hotspots"      value="$(arg num_hotspots)" />
    <param name="world_half_size"   value="$(arg world_half_size)" />
    <param name="collect_dist"      value="$(arg collect_dist)" />
    <param name="suppress_altitude" value="$(arg suppress_altitude)" />
    <param name="min_drop_altitude" value="$(arg min_drop_altitude)" />
    <param name="work_per_intensity" value="$(arg work_per_intensity)" />
    <param name="max_crew"          value="$(arg max_crew)" />
    <param name="join_deadline"     value="$(arg join_deadline)" />
    <param name="crew_separation"   value="$(arg crew_separation)" />
    <param name="crew_altitude_step" value="$(arg crew_altitude_step)" />
    <!-- Where the first crew member holds, and what the others are stacked on
         top of. Must match the suppressor's own drop_altitude, which is what a
         drone falls back to if it is ever sent a station-less tasking. -->
    <param name="drop_altitude"     value="$(arg drop_altitude)" />
    <!-- Cap on the ring radius. 0.75 keeps a full crew inside collect_dist
         (1.00) with margin; the chord formula only reaches it past a crew of
         four. -->
    <param name="max_ring"          value="0.75" />
    <param name="commit_timeout"    value="$(arg commit_timeout)" />
    <!-- Keep fires apart. This has to clear two *crews* now, not two drones: a
         full crew occupies a ring of up to 0.75 m plus a rotor span, so fires
         closer than about 2 m would have their crews flying through each
         other. It also stops a cluster inside one bombing radius letting a
         drone work several fires from one station. -->
    <param name="min_separation"    value="2.5" />
    <param name="extinguish_pause"  value="$(arg extinguish_pause)" />
    <param name="flame_scale"       value="$(arg flame_scale)" />
    <param name="fire_light"        value="$(arg fire_light)" type="bool" />
    <param name="flicker_hz"        value="$(arg flicker_hz)" />
    <param name="publish_hz"        value="10.0" />
    <!-- A slow backstop only, and it skips any fire that has a crew: moving a
         fire with drones committed to it strands a whole coalition rather than
         one drone. -->
    <param name="reignite_period"   value="120.0" />
    <param name="obstacle_clearance" value="0.8" />
    <rosparam file="$(arg obstacle_file)" command="load" />
  </node>

  <node if="$(arg collide)" pkg="uav_bushfire" type="collision_monitor"
        name="collision_monitor" output="screen">
    <rosparam file="$(arg obstacle_file)" command="load" />
    <param name="rotor_radius" value="0.40" />
    <param name="drone_vsep"   value="0.30" />
    <param name="tree_height"  value="3.00" />
  </node>

</launch>
